Try it: MA(1) explorer





viewof theta = Inputs.range([-1.5, 1.5], {
  value: 0.8, step: 0.05, label: "MA(1) coefficient θ"
})









rho1 = theta / (1 + theta * theta)
Hmax = 8
acfData = d3.range(0, Hmax + 1).map(h =&gt; ({
  lag: h, rho: h === 0 ? 1 : (h === 1 ? rho1 : 0)
}))

Plot.plot({
  height: 300, marginLeft: 45,
  x: {label: "lag h", domain: [-0.5, Hmax + 0.5]},
  y: {label: "ρ(h)", domain: [-0.6, 1.05], grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY([0]),
    Plot.ruleX(acfData, {x: "lag", y1: 0, y2: "rho", stroke: "#1b3a6b", strokeWidth: 3}),
    Plot.dot(acfData, {x: "lag", y: "rho", fill: "#1b3a6b", r: 4})
  ]
})






























html`&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;div class="muted"&gt;Lag-1 autocorrelation &nbsp; ρ₁ = θ / (1 + θ²)&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="posterior"&gt;${rho1.toFixed(3)}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="note"&gt;
    |θ| = ${Math.abs(theta).toFixed(2)} ⇒
    &lt;b&gt;${Math.abs(theta) &lt; 1 ? "invertible" : (Math.abs(theta) &gt; 1 ? "not invertible" : "unit root")}&lt;/b&gt;
    &nbsp;·&nbsp; ACF cuts off after lag 1
    &nbsp;·&nbsp; note |ρ₁| ≤ 0.5, reached at θ = ±1
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;`








Move \(\theta\): the sign of \(\rho_1\) follows the sign of \(\theta\), its magnitude never exceeds \(0.5\), and \(\theta\) vs \(1/\theta\) give the same \(\rho_1\) — the invertibility ambiguity.

Control structures
Control structures direct the flow of execution through a program. R’s main ones are if/else for choosing between branches, for and while for repetition, and the less common repeat. The sections below take them in turn.

if and else
An if statement runs a block of code only when a condition is TRUE, and an optional else block runs otherwise. The condition must evaluate to a single TRUE or FALSE value.
if (condition) {
  ## runs when condition is TRUE
} else {
  ## runs when condition is FALSE
}


Show R code
x &lt;- 3

if (x &gt; 5) {
  print("x is greater than 5")
} else {
  print("x is 5 or less")
}


[1] "x is 5 or less"




Show R code
fun1 &lt;- function(x){if (x &gt; 50) {
  print("x is greater than 50")
} else {
  print("x is 50 or less")
}}




Show R code
fun1(51)


[1] "x is greater than 50"




Nested if
Because the body of an else can contain another if, you can nest conditions to any depth to distinguish more than two cases.
if (condition1) {
  ## expression
} else {
  if (condition2) {
    ## expression
  } else {
    ## expression
  }
}


Show R code
x &lt;- 7

if (x &gt; 10) {
  print("x &gt; 10")
} else {
  if (x &gt; 5) {
    print("5 &lt; x &lt;= 10")
  } else {
    print("x &lt;= 5")
  }
}

Key takeaways
You should now be able to:

distinguish a stochastic process from one observed realization;
explain covariance stationarity, and ergodicity as the equality of time and ensemble averages;
give an economic or financial example where ergodicity fails (permanent income; a multiplicative bet);
interpret population autocovariances and autocorrelations;
distinguish white noise from independence;
explain Wold innovations and their role in linear forecasting;
derive the ACF of MA(1) and MA(\(q\)) processes;
explain why an MA(\(q\)) ACF cuts off after lag \(q\);
distinguish stationarity from invertibility and check the MA root condition.

Try it: base rate matters





viewof prev = Inputs.range([0.001, 0.5], {
  value: 0.01, step: 0.001, label: "Prevalence"
})

viewof sens = Inputs.range([0.50, 1.00], {
  value: 0.99, step: 0.005, label: "Sensitivity"
})

viewof fpr = Inputs.range([0.00, 0.50], {
  value: 0.05, step: 0.005, label: "False positive"
})

























N = 100000
diseased = Math.round(prev * N)
healthy  = N - diseased
tp = Math.round(sens * diseased)
fp = Math.round(fpr * healthy)
posterior = tp / (tp + fp)

html`
&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;div class="muted"&gt;Posterior probability: P(D | +)&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="posterior"&gt;${(posterior * 100).toFixed(1)}%&lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;table&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Test +&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Test −&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disease&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td style="color:#1b3a6b"&gt;&lt;b&gt;${tp.toLocaleString()}&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;${(diseased - tp).toLocaleString()}&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;&lt;b&gt;Healthy&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td style="color:#c0392b"&gt;&lt;b&gt;${fp.toLocaleString()}&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;${(healthy - fp).toLocaleString()}&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/table&gt;

  &lt;div class="note"&gt;
    Out of ${N.toLocaleString()} people, ${diseased.toLocaleString()} have the disease.
    Of ${Number(tp + fp).toLocaleString()} positive tests,
    ${tp.toLocaleString()} are true positives.
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;`




















































Move only prevalence first. The test is unchanged, but the posterior changes sharply.

Try it: prior meets data





viewof priorMean = Inputs.range([0.02, 0.50], {
  value: 0.10, step: 0.005, label: "Prior mean"
})

viewof priorStrength = Inputs.range([2, 200], {
  value: 20, step: 1, label: "Prior strength a+b"
})

viewof kObs = Inputs.range([0, 50], {
  value: 8, step: 1, label: "Defaults k (of n = 50)"
})

























nObs = 50
aPrior = priorMean * priorStrength
bPrior = (1 - priorMean) * priorStrength
aPost  = aPrior + kObs
bPost  = bPrior + (nObs - kObs)
postMean = aPost / (aPost + bPost)

// Unnormalised Beta density; normalise numerically on the grid
xs = d3.range(0.001, 0.6, 0.002)
dx = 0.002
betaU = (x, a, b) =&gt; Math.exp((a - 1) * Math.log(x) + (b - 1) * Math.log(1 - x))
normalise = (a, b) =&gt; {
  const raw = xs.map(x =&gt; betaU(x, a, b));
  const area = d3.sum(raw) * dx;
  return xs.map((x, i) =&gt; ({x, y: raw[i] / area}));
}

priorCurve = normalise(aPrior, bPrior)
postCurve  = normalise(aPost, bPost)
likeCurve  = normalise(kObs + 1, nObs - kObs + 1)   // likelihood shape in p

// P(p &gt; 0.10 | data) by numeric integration of the posterior
probAbove = d3.sum(postCurve.filter(d =&gt; d.x &gt; 0.10).map(d =&gt; d.y)) * dx

Plot.plot({
  height: 300, marginLeft: 45,
  x: {label: "Default probability p", domain: [0, 0.55]},
  y: {label: "density", grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.line(priorCurve, {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "#8aa0c2", strokeWidth: 2, strokeDasharray: "4 3"}),
    Plot.line(likeCurve,  {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "#c0392b", strokeWidth: 1.5, strokeDasharray: "1 3"}),
    Plot.line(postCurve,  {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "#1b3a6b", strokeWidth: 3}),
    Plot.ruleX([postMean], {stroke: "#1b3a6b", strokeWidth: 1}),
    Plot.ruleY([0])
  ]
})











































































































html`&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;div class="muted"&gt;Posterior mean E(p | data)&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="posterior"&gt;${(postMean * 100).toFixed(1)}%&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="note"&gt;
    &lt;b style="color:#8aa0c2"&gt;╌&lt;/b&gt; prior &nbsp;·&nbsp;
    &lt;b style="color:#c0392b"&gt;┈&lt;/b&gt; likelihood &nbsp;·&nbsp;
    &lt;b style="color:#1b3a6b"&gt;━&lt;/b&gt; posterior &nbsp; Beta(${aPost.toFixed(1)}, ${bPost.toFixed(1)}) &nbsp;·&nbsp;
    P(p &gt; 0.10 | data) ≈ &lt;b&gt;${(probAbove * 100).toFixed(0)}%&lt;/b&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;`








A stronger prior pulls the posterior toward the prior mean; more data (or the same \(k\) with the shape sharpened) lets the likelihood dominate.

Sampling distribution of the slope
\(\beta_1\) is a fixed but unknown parameter. The estimate \(\hat\beta_1\) comes from this sample; across repeated samples it would vary — its sampling distribution. From it we obtain:

a standard error for \(\hat\beta_1\);
a confidence interval for \(\beta_1\);
a test of, say, \(H_0:\beta_1=0\).


In a log-wage regression the slope is approximately the proportional difference in wage associated with one more year of education.
